**Dual-write (phase 1).** Legacy `pg-orders-1` stays the source of truth for every read and write. Each write also commits an `orders_outbox` row in the same transaction, and `outbox-relay` (6 replicas, idempotent on `(order_id, version)`) replays those rows into orders-v2 in commit order. Meanwhile, `backfill_orders_v2` copies historical orders each night from 01:00 to 05:00 UTC, which takes about 11 nights, and `order-diff` compares the two stores every hour.

Nothing reads from orders-v2 yet, so customers can't see its errors. The phase passes when four things hold:

- the backfill is complete;
- the one-time full-table checksum matches;
- the mismatch rate stays under 0.01% for 3 consecutive days;
- relay lag p99 stays under 2 seconds for those same 3 days.

**Cutover (phase 2).** The `orders.read_from_v2` flag moves reads to orders-v2 in steps of 1%, 10%, 50% and 100%, one step a day, while writes still go to both stores. Reads then stay at 100% for a 48-hour soak. After that, `orders.write_legacy` is turned off, which makes orders-v2 the only store receiving writes, and the relay is scaled to zero. Legacy stays up read-only for 30 days and is then decommissioned under `CHG-4471`. The cutover counts as done after 7 days with no `ORD-4xx` incident and order-history p99 at or under 180 ms.

**The write stop can't be undone.** Before it, rolling back just means setting `orders.read_from_v2` to 0, and nothing is lost because legacy has received every write. After it, nothing syncs from orders-v2 back to legacy, so going back to legacy would lose every order written since. That's why the write stop waits for the soak and needs sign-off from the DBA lead and the commerce director. If it misses 2026-11-30, the code freeze pushes it to January.

**Dual-write (phase 1).** Legacy `pg-orders-1` stays the source of truth for every read and write. Each write also commits an `orders_outbox` row in the same legacy transaction, and `outbox-relay` applies those rows to orders-v2 in commit order. It is idempotent on `(order_id, version)`, so replays are safe. Meanwhile `backfill_orders_v2` copies historical orders nightly, and nothing reads orders-v2 yet, so its errors are invisible to customers. Four conditions must hold before the cutover can start:

- the backfill is complete;
- `order-diff` mismatches stay under 0.01% for 3 days;
- relay lag p99 stays under 2 s over the same 3 days;
- a one-time full-table checksum matches, because the hourly diff only samples old orders.

**Cutover (phase 2).** `orders.read_from_v2` moves reads over at 1%, 10%, 50% and 100%, one step a day, while writes still go to both stores. Reads then soak at 100% for 48 hours. Until this point, rollback is just setting the flag back to 0, and nothing is lost. Turning off `orders.write_legacy` is the point of no return. Legacy stops receiving writes, and there is no sync from orders-v2 back to it, so falling back would lose every order written after that. That's why this step needs sign-off from the DBA lead and the commerce director. Legacy then stays up read-only for 30 days before it's decommissioned under `CHG-4471`.

The timeline has no slack. The four ramp days (11-24 to 11-27) plus the 48-hour soak end exactly at the 11-30 write stop. If any ramp step is held, the write stop misses 11-30 and is pushed past the code freeze into January. Dual-write would keep running at full cost until then.
